# ---------------- Boss.events (Task 21 Part D) ----------------
# A scripted boss with no C#: it wakes, then attacks close or ranged at random, and every third
# attack it breaks off, runs to one of the authored hide points, and comes back. A close attack
# comes in from one of the player's own attack points, so the swing can arrive from the front,
# the back or a flank. Its health picks the phase: at 200 it summons an add and starts calling
# in a random lane enemy every few seconds (up to four), and at 100 it enrages — its throw turns
# into a three-knife fan.
#
# The whole fight is the arcade lane's grammar — `set state` / `when state is`, `chance` +
# `otherwise`, `wait` as a telegraph, `goto` to move, `spawn` to summon — plus the two Task 34
# progress-bar words for the bar at the top of the screen. Nothing below needs code.
#
# SETUP (editor), one scene:
#   1. `Boss` template: an inactive actor named `Boss` with an EventSheet (Event File = Boss)
#      and a BoxCollider. The name is the type — `enemy type my name` reads it, so
#      `kills Boss` counts separately from `kills Zombie`. Its stats come from
#      EnemyTypes.events (`BossSpeed`, `BossHealth`, ...). Spawn it with
#      `spawn Boss at BossSpawn` from the arena's own sheet when the fight should start.
#   2. Hide points: two or more EMPTY actors, each named exactly `HidePoint`, spread across the
#      floor (Flax allows duplicate names). `goto random HidePoint` picks one per retreat —
#      `random <thing>` needs the shared name, so `HidePoint1`/`HidePoint2` would not work.
#   2b. Attack points: three or more EMPTY actors named exactly `AttackPoint`, PARENTED to the
#      player actor and placed around it — one in front, one behind, one to each side, a couple of
#      metres out and level with the floor. `goto random AttackPoint` picks one per close attack,
#      so the boss comes at you from a different side each time. Ground level matters: `goto`
#      walks the floor, so a point floating above the player is flattened onto the ground plane —
#      an aerial entrance needs `jump`, not an empty. Missing points only warn (the boss still
#      walks in and swings), so a bare test scene keeps working.
#   3. BossBar: a UIControl actor named `BossBar` whose Control is a ProgressBar, placed where
#      the bar should sit, inactive at scene start. `show BossBar` reveals it, `hide BossBar`
#      drops it on death, and `set progress of BossBar to ...` fills it.
#   4. BossBullet: the ranged attack's projectile, a template like `Bullet` (dynamic body, no
#      gravity, CCD, a trigger collider) with its own sheet that flies along ITS OWN facing —
#      `move forward speed my BulletSpeed`, the way TK_Bullet does — and sends `"hurt"` on
#      contact. It is never told where the player is. That is what makes aiming possible at all:
#      `spawn` copies the spawn spot's orientation onto the clone, so the knife goes wherever the
#      spot it was spawned at is pointed. Spawned straight at `me`, it leaves along the boss's own
#      facing (a throw aimed by which way the boss faces); spawned at the rotated `Knife*` empties
#      in 4b, the same sheet gives the fan. The boss sheet only spawns it — a BossBullet sheet that
#      homed on the player would collapse the fan back into one direction and break 4b.
#   4b. Knife fan points: three EMPTY actors named EXACTLY `KnifeCenter`, `KnifeLeft` and
#      `KnifeRight`, PARENTED to the boss and placed a little in front of it, level with the
#      floor. Rotate the two outer ones ~15-20 degrees out from the centre line. This is the
#      whole fan: because the clone inherits the spot's orientation and flies along its own
#      facing (4), three rotated empties throw one knife straight ahead and two off to the sides.
#      Their forward axis also follows the boss (it turns to face the player), so the fan always
#      points where the boss is aiming. `spawn ... at KnifeLeft` with no such
#      empty in the scene only warns and that knife never appears, so a bare test scene falls
#      back to one or two knives instead of breaking the fight.
#   5. Optional clips on the Boss model: Roar (wake-up), Walk (chasing and returning),
#      Run (the retreat), Attack (the close swing), Throw (the ranged telegraph), Flinch (a hit)
#      and Die (the death). A name the model does not have warns once and the rest of the block
#      still runs, so a placeholder mesh can play the whole fight.
#
# NO BUSY GUARD IN `when every`: a polled event re-fires every tick while its conditions hold,
# and a `wait` suspends only that one run, so the attack blocks open with `my Attacking is 0`,
# set it to 1, and put it back to 0 at the end of BOTH branches. Without the flag the boss would
# start a second attack every 1.5 s while the first is still waiting.

when me appears
  enemy type my name                  # Boss: speed/sight/health come from EnemyTypes.events
  set my MaxHealth to my Health        # so `add -10 to my Health` clamps at 300
  set my EnragedSpeed to my Speed * 2  # worked out once, so enraging twice cannot compound
  set my Phase to 1
  set my Attacks to 0
  set my Attacking to 0
  set progress of BossBar to 100
  show BossBar
  set state wake
  print "Boss: awake"

# ---- waking up: one roar, one shake, then the fight ------------------------------------
when state is wake
  play anim "Roar"
  shake camera strength 12 for 0.8 seconds
  wait 1.2 seconds
  my state is "wake"                  # not deleted or reset mid-roar? start fighting
  set state fight

# ---- the bar follows the health ---------------------------------------------------------
# `set progress of` takes a number, not a sum, so the percentage is worked out on the line above
# it: 300 HP is 100%, and the `+ 1` keeps the number above 1 even at 1 HP — the bridge reads any
# value over 1 as a percentage, so a bare 1 would paint a full bar. A `bind` cannot do this job:
# it hands the bar `my Health` itself (300).
when my Health changes
  set my HealthPct to my Health * 100 / 300 + 1
  set progress of BossBar to my HealthPct

# ---- phases: each threshold is crossed once, from above ---------------------------------
# `when my Health is lower than <n>` is the health edge, not a level test: it fires on the tick
# health crosses the number from above and re-arms if healing takes it back over.
when my Health is lower than 200
  set my Phase to 2
  set state retreat
  spawn Knife at me offset 90         # the add: a lane Knife joins the fight
  shake camera strength 8
  print "Boss: phase 2 — summoning help"

when my Health is lower than 100
  set my Phase to 3
  # Assign, never multiply: this health edge re-arms if healing lifts the boss back over 100,
  # so `set my Speed to my Speed * 2` would double the speed again on the next crossing.
  # `my EnragedSpeed` was derived from the type's own pace in `me appears`.
  set my Speed to my EnragedSpeed
  set state retreat
  shake camera strength 14 for 0.6 seconds
  print "Boss: enraged"

# ---- phase 1-2 attack: close or ranged, at random ---------------------------------------
# `chance` re-rolls on every poll, so the roll sits inside a block that the `my Attacking` flag
# lets run once per attack. The counters and the flag go BEFORE the branch on purpose: an
# `otherwise` body runs only when its condition failed and its gate holds until the next
# condition line, so shared cleanup written after the branch would be swallowed by the else.
when every 1.5 seconds
  my state is "fight"
  my Phase is lower than 3
  my Attacking is 0
  set my Attacking to 1
  add 1 to my Attacks
  chance 50%
  # close: sidestep to one of the player's attack points first, then close and swing — the swing
  # starts before its wait, so the hit lands partway through the clip instead of on the tick the
  # clip begins. The second `goto` matters: a point can be far enough out that the swing would
  # otherwise whiff.
  goto random AttackPoint speed my Speed facing forward
  loop anim "Walk"
  wait 0.3 seconds
  my state is "fight"                # a health gate broke us off mid-stride? no swing
  goto nearest player speed my Speed facing forward
  play anim "Attack"
  wait 0.4 seconds
  send message "hurt" to nearest player
  set my Attacking to 0
  otherwise
  # ranged: stop, telegraph the throw, then fire
  stop moving
  play anim "Throw"
  wait 0.5 seconds
  my state is "fight"                # broke off during the wind-up? no shot
  spawn BossBullet at me offset 40
  set my Attacking to 0

# ---- phase 3: the same two attacks, faster and doubled ----------------------------------
when every 0.8 seconds
  my state is "fight"
  my Phase is 3
  my Attacking is 0
  set my Attacking to 1
  add 1 to my Attacks
  chance 60%
  # the same flank, run at the enraged speed
  goto random AttackPoint speed my Speed facing forward
  loop anim "Run"
  wait 0.25 seconds
  my state is "fight"                # as above, at the enraged pace
  goto nearest player speed my Speed facing forward
  play anim "Attack"
  wait 0.3 seconds
  send message "hurt" to nearest player
  set my Attacking to 0
  otherwise
  stop moving
  play anim "Throw"
  wait 0.35 seconds
  my state is "fight"                # as above, for the whole fan
  # the fan: one knife ahead and two off the sides, from the three rotated empties. The short
  # stagger makes it read as a thrown fan instead of a volley fired from one point — and a hit
  # that lands between them cancels the rest, because each `wait` is followed by the state guard.
  spawn BossBullet at KnifeCenter offset 40
  wait 0.08 seconds
  my state is "fight"
  spawn BossBullet at KnifeLeft offset 40
  wait 0.08 seconds
  my state is "fight"
  spawn BossBullet at KnifeRight offset 40
  set my Attacking to 0

# ---- adds: once the health is down, the room joins in -----------------------------------
# A random *type* cannot be written as one word: `random <name>` picks among actors that share a
# name, and `spawn` takes a single template. So the sheet rolls with a chance ladder — a failed
# `chance` hands over to the branch under it, and each `otherwise` re-points the gate at the next
# roll. The standing distance is a value, so it rolls too (`random 110 to 170`) and two adds never
# land on the same tile. `my Summons` caps the help at four: a long fight must not fill the room.
# The state guard sits after the ladder on purpose: statements behind a failed `otherwise` are
# skipped until the next condition line, and the guard is one.
when every 5 seconds
  my state is "fight"
  my Attacking is 0                  # never cut a swing short: the roar waits for a gap
  my Health is lower than 200
  my Summons is lower than 4
  set state summon

when state is summon
  add 1 to my Summons
  set my Reach to random 110 to 170
  play anim "Roar"                    # the roar is the add's entrance cue
  shake camera strength 7
  print "Boss: summoned help"
  chance 34%
  spawn Pistolboy at me offset my Reach
  otherwise
  chance 50%
  spawn UziRebel at me offset my Reach
  otherwise
  spawn ShotgunManiac at me offset my Reach
  my state is "summon"                # killed mid-summon? the adds stay, the boss does not move on
  set state fight

# ---- after three attacks: break off -----------------------------------------------------
when every 0.25 seconds
  my state is "fight"
  my Attacks is 3
  my Attacking is 0
  set state retreat

# ---- the retreat itself ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Walk to a hide point, wait there (the boss is untouchable behind its own timing, not by a new
# rule), then walk back in. Phase 3 needs no branch here: the enrage gate doubled my Speed, so
# the same `goto` is a dash. A `wait` never cancels another event, hence the state re-check
# after each one — a phase change or a death mid-retreat must not walk the boss back to life.
when state is retreat
  set my Attacks to 0
  # An attack run that a health gate interrupted is skipped by its own `my state is "fight"`
  # guard, so the flag it set would stay at 1 and no attack would ever start again. The state
  # that took over owns the reset.
  set my Attacking to 0
  stop moving
  goto random HidePoint speed my Speed facing forward
  loop anim "Run"
  wait 1.5 seconds
  my state is "retreat"
  goto nearest player speed my Speed facing forward
  loop anim "Walk"
  wait 0.2 seconds
  my state is "retreat"
  set state fight

# ---- taking a hit ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The lane's own hit shape, reused rather than a second system: damage always lands, and the
# flinch is gated so a burst cannot stun-lock the boss out of its attack.
when me touches Bullet
  add -10 to my Health
  destroy other
  add 1 to Combo
  my Attacking is 0
  my state is "fight"
  set state flinch

when message "hurt" arrives
  add -10 to my Health
  add 1 to Combo

when state is flinch
  set my Attacking to 0                 # a hit that cancelled a swing owns the flag reset too
  stop moving
  play anim "Flinch"
  wait 0.25 seconds
  my state is "flinch"                # not hit again during the flinch? straight back in
  set state fight

# ---- death ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `kills my Type` counts Boss in its own bucket, so a level's `kills Boss is 1` never mixes
# with the lane's counters.
when my Health becomes 0
  hide BossBar
  set state dying                    # one state closes every fight block, so no late swing lands
  set my Attacking to 0
  add 1 to kills my Type
  play anim "Die"
  wait 0.8 seconds                   # let the clip play before the body goes
  spawn Explosion at me
  destroy me
  print "Boss: down"
